\section*{A Laughlin gap stable under every small bounded scalar perturbation}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. The expanding-sphere Laughlin gap and unique ground state are claimed stable under small bounded projected scalar disorder, uniformly over profiles and particle number.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
A local lowest-Landau-level calculus tracks pinned zeros and weighted observables. Orthogonal branch readout, charge bounds, and rotation averaging control local removal of interaction energy; the unperturbed Fock-space gap then absorbs the bounded perturbation.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The expanding-sphere Laughlin gap and unique ground state are claimed stable under small bounded projected scalar disorder, uniformly over profiles and particle number. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 269, the documented entries give unperturbed spherical Laughlin and stronger Fock-space gap inequalities. Stability against scalar one-body disorder and uniqueness of the perturbed ground state are not included. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{Laughlin.lean}, \texttt{LaughlinFock.lean}, \texttt{LaughlinGap.lean}, \texttt{LaughlinPlanar.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.Laughlin.mainTarget\_proved}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

